# Copy to .env — never commit .env
# Local dev: `npx prisma dev` (local Prisma Postgres) or any Postgres instance
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/aidriver"

# --- Real-database tests (optional locally, automatic in CI) ---
# Overrides DATABASE_URL for the tests that CANNOT run against a fake store,
# because what they test is an interaction between two tables' constraints
# inside one transaction: src/modules/payments/__tests__/poison-pill.postgres
# .test.ts, which pins the replay-after-revoke defect that used to 500 the
# Stripe webhook for three days after an ordinary support action.
#
# Point it at any Postgres with the migrations applied:
#   createdb knijka_test
#   DATABASE_URL=postgresql://…/knijka_test npx prisma migrate deploy
#   PAYMENTS_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://…/knijka_test npm test
#
# Unset and with no migrated DATABASE_URL, those tests SKIP with a printed
# reason — never silently. CI sets DATABASE_URL and applies migrations before
# vitest, so they always run there, and the file FAILS rather than skips if
# the database is missing in CI. Every row it writes is prefixed per run and
# dropped afterwards, so a shared development database is safe to point at.
PAYMENTS_TEST_DATABASE_URL=""

# Claude API (tutor/debrief layer — ADR-002)
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=""
# Hard daily ceiling on what the tutor may spend on that key, in US dollars
# (audit H-8). Optional — the default in src/modules/tutor/budget.ts applies
# when unset, and an unparseable value falls back to it rather than to zero.
# Past the ceiling the tutor answers in Bulgarian that it is out for the day;
# it never errors, and practice + explanations keep working.
# Sized for ~3,500 answers/day. Do NOT raise it further without checking that
# the per-pack allowance (payments/quota.ts) is still in force — this ceiling
# is a site-wide brownout switch, not a per-user brake (doc 81 §5.2-5.3).
TUTOR_DAILY_BUDGET_USD="40"

# Auth secret (generate: openssl rand -base64 32)
# Also derives the practice-session ticket key (see below) — one root secret.
AUTH_SECRET=""

# --- Kill switch (src/lib/features.ts) ---
# Turns a feature off WITHOUT a deploy. Accepts any of `simulator`, `hazard`,
# `tutor`, comma- or space-separated; empty (this file's value) disables
# nothing. On the box: append it to /opt/knijka/platform/.env and
# `pm2 restart knijka --update-env` — no code change, no CI run, no five-minute
# deploy tick while students are hitting the thing that is on fire.
#
# It is read at CALL TIME and checked in the page guard AND in the server
# actions, because a page guard alone leaves the POST endpoints live and the
# endpoints are where the money goes (model tokens, physics sessions, writes).
#
# An unrecognised name disables NOTHING — `DISABLED_FEATURES=simulater` looks
# like it worked and leaves every student driving. /admin prints the parsed
# value, unknown tokens in red; verify there, not here. Deliberately not on
# /api/health, which is public and reports no configuration at all.
DISABLED_FEATURES=""

# --- Practice session binding (audit M-10) ---
# The practice submit action answers with the full key (correctOptionIds,
# explanation, citations). Its only input used to be a question id, so ANY id in
# the bank could be exchanged for its answers — including the 45 ids sitting in
# the DOM of a mock exam open in another tab. Worse than the cheating: every
# forged answer wrote a QuestionAttempt and moved mastery, which made the
# readiness score client-writable.
#
# The fix is a signed ticket over the questions the engine actually dealt. This
# variable only decides what happens when NO ticket is presented at all:
#   "1" / "true"  → required (what this file ships, and what the app runs on)
#   "0" / "false" → BREAK GLASS. Grades unbound submissions again, no rebuild
#                   needed, for the evening the wiring turns out to be wrong.
#   unset         → required in production, permissive elsewhere, so a forgotten
#                   variable on the VPS fails SAFE rather than silently
#                   re-opening the oracle.
PRACTICE_TICKET_REQUIRED="1"

# --- Founder seed (scripts/seed-founder.mjs) ---
# The password for founder@knijka.ai, which is created with role=admin — and
# admin bypasses the simulator entitlement, the mock-exam limit, the practice
# cap, the hazard gate and the tutor quota. This repo is PUBLIC, so the script
# has no default: without this variable set it refuses to create the account
# and exits non-zero. It also refuses to run against a non-localhost
# DATABASE_URL or with NODE_ENV=production — seeding an admin into a live
# database is an ops action, not a script anyone can run by accident.
# Leave EMPTY here; set it in your own .env, never in a committed file.
SEED_FOUNDER_PASSWORD=""

# --- Payments (Stripe, one-time packs — src/modules/payments) ---
# Without STRIPE_SECRET_KEY the app still runs: /pricing shows the packs with
# disabled "Скоро" buttons and the webhook answers 503. No crashes.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=""
# Publishable key — client-side, required for the embedded /checkout page
# (renders card + Apple Pay + Revolut Pay). pk_test_… / pk_live_…
NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=""
# Signing secret of the /api/stripe/webhook endpoint (whsec_..., from the
# Stripe dashboard → Developers → Webhooks, or `stripe listen` in dev).
# REQUIRED to sell: without it isStripeConfigured() is false and the buy
# buttons stay disabled — because the webhook is the only path that fulfils a
# purchase when the buyer closes the tab or pays by a delayed method.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""
# What this deployment believes it is: "test" or "live". Anything else (and
# absent) is read as "test", because a deployment that has not declared itself
# live must not take real money.
#
# It exists to catch the MIXED configuration — most often a live sk_live_ key
# paired with the whsec_ of the TEST endpoint, since the two are copied from
# different Dashboard screens. Every real webhook then fails signature
# verification, the route answers 400, Stripe treats 400 as permanent and never
# retries: every live purchase charged and unfulfilled. The webhook also
# rejects any event whose `livemode` disagrees with this value.
STRIPE_MODE="test"
# Absolute base URL of this deployment — used for Stripe success/cancel
# redirects AND for the password-reset link in the e-mail below. A wrong value
# here means every reset link 404s, so set it per environment.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# --- Transactional e-mail (src/modules/mail) ---
# Today the product needs exactly one e-mail: the password-reset link (audit
# H-14). The flow is COMPLETE and tested without any of the variables below —
# with MAIL_TRANSPORT unset it uses the console transport and prints the reset
# link into the server log (`npm run dev` locally, `pm2 logs knijka` on the
# VPS), so a locked-out student can be recovered today by reading it out.
#
# REQUIRED TO SELL. isStripeConfigured() is false while these are unset, so the
# buy buttons stay disabled — for the same reason the legal-identity placeholders
# disable them. Taking EUR 12.99 from a seventeen-year-old in September and
# having no way to send her a reset link in October means she is locked out of
# something she paid for with no channel to a human. The product refuses to sell
# what it cannot give back. /api/health reports the same state under
# `checks.mail` (transport + ok), and the server log names the missing variables.
#
# TO TURN ON REAL DELIVERY — this is the only remaining step, no code change:
#   1. Open an account at https://resend.com or https://postmarkapp.com
#      (both have a free tier that covers a Bulgarian launch; either works).
#   2. Verify the sending domain there (SPF + DKIM DNS records) — an unverified
#      sender is what puts account-recovery mail in the spam folder.
#   3. Set the three variables below and restart the app. That is all.
#
# console (default) | resend | postmark. A provider name with missing
# credentials falls back to console with a warning — never a crash, because a
# mail misconfiguration must not also break the recovery page.
MAIL_TRANSPORT="console"
# Provider API key: Resend `re_...` / Postmark server token. Required by both
# providers; ignored by the console transport.
MAIL_API_KEY=""
# Sender, on the domain verified in step 2. Display name is shown to students.
# Required for a provider (they reject an unverified/absent From).
MAIL_FROM="Книжка.AI <no-reply@example.bg>"
# Optional: where a student's reply goes. Leave empty to send no Reply-To.
MAIL_REPLY_TO=""

# --- Tutor voice (pre-rendered TTS — tools/theory/synthesize_bg.mjs, doc 81 §3.3) ---
# BUILD-TIME ONLY. Nothing in the app calls a TTS API at runtime, by design:
# the bytes a student hears are a fixed rendering of approved, lawRefs-cited
# content, so no model can free-recall Bulgarian law into a student's ears
# (ADR-002). Leaving every value below empty is the correct state today — the
# script then runs its dry-run transport and spends nothing.
#
# TWO independent brakes, both required before a single paid call is made:
# credentials here AND `--allow-spend` on the command line. `--plan` prints the
# bill first (today: 5,962 files, 646,083 chars, $9.69 at the westeurope meter).
#
# DO NOT ARM THIS before the §3.4 voice audition — no bg-BG voice has yet been
# heard by a native ear, and shipping captions-only is an acceptable outcome.
# azure | dryrun. Anything else falls back to dryrun with a printed reason.
TUTOR_TTS_PROVIDER="azure"
TUTOR_TTS_VOICE="bg-BG-KalinaNeural"
# Azure portal → Speech resource. The region matters: the $15/1M price above was
# read from westeurope's sheet, and another region is another price sheet.
AZURE_SPEECH_KEY=""
AZURE_SPEECH_REGION="westeurope"
